00:51:14,120 --> 00:51:17,120
I thought this lecture would start with something about the

1057
00:51:17,120 --> 00:51:18,120
theory of probability. But I'm not

1058
00:51:18,120 --> 00:51:19,120
going to get into that very much.

1059
00:51:19,120 --> 00:51:21,120
That will be more for a

1060
00:51:21,120 --> 00:51:23,120
TA section that will come in later.

1061
00:51:23,120 --> 00:51:25,120
But even so, this is not a probability course.

1062
00:51:25,120 --> 00:51:27,120
I just want to kind of

1063
00:51:27,120 --> 00:51:29,120
remind you of concepts of probability.

1064
00:51:29,120 --> 00:51:32,120
And there's a concept of independent risks.

1065
00:51:32,120 --> 00:51:35,120
If risks are independent, you can diversify away them.

1066
00:51:35,120 --> 00:51:38,120
And you can put together

1067
00:51:38,120 --> 00:51:41,120
a portfolio that minimizes the risks.

1068
00:51:41,120 --> 00:51:43,120
The law of large numbers says, if

1069
00:51:43,120 --> 00:51:45,120
you have a lot of independent risks,

1070
00:51:45,120 --> 00:51:47,120
they'll average out if you have

1071
00:51:47,120 --> 00:51:49,120
a large number of these different risks.

1072
00:51:49,120 --> 00:51:53,120
In your portfolio. And there's no risk left.

1073
00:51:53,120 --> 00:51:55,120
That's if they're independent.

1074
00:51:55,120 --> 00:51:57,120
But in fact, risks are

1075
00:51:57,120 --> 00:51:59,120
not as independent as you think.

1076
00:51:59,120 --> 00:52:03,120
And that's one reason why we had a financial crisis.

1077
00:52:03,120 --> 00:52:05,120
So, a lot of people were

1078
00:52:05,120 --> 00:52:07,120
making plans based on portfolio theory

1079
00:52:07,120 --> 00:52:09,120
in finance, but the plans assumed

1080
00:52:09,120 --> 00:52:11,120
that there won't be a crisis.

1081
00:52:11,120 --> 00:52:13,120
That maybe one of our investments will

1082
00:52:13,120 --> 00:52:15,120
go bad, but they can't all go bad.

1083
00:52:15,120 --> 00:52:17,120
Or a large number of them can't go bad.

1084
00:52:17,120 --> 00:52:19,120
So, that was a failure of the Internet.

1085
00:52:19,120 --> 00:52:23,120
The independence assumption in finance.

1086
00:52:23,120 --> 00:52:26,120
That failure created the financial

1087
00:52:26,120 --> 00:52:29,120
crisis that we've been through.

1088
00:52:29,120 --> 00:52:34,120
It was a near miss onto another Great Depression.

1089
00:52:34,120 --> 00:52:37,120
The financial crisis that began in 1929,

1090
00:52:37,120 --> 00:52:40,120
I'll talk about that briefly in that lecture,

1091
00:52:40,120 --> 00:52:44,120
started with the stock market crash of 1929.

1092
00:52:44,120 --> 00:52:47,120
And the economy spiraled down until 1934.

1093
00:52:47,120 --> 00:52:49,120
And then, in 1935, the economy went down.

1094
00:52:49,120 --> 00:52:52,120
And then, in 1933, it

1095
00:52:52,120 --> 00:52:55,120
just kept getting worse and worse.

1096
00:52:55,120 --> 00:52:59,120
More and more bankruptcies, more and more layoffs.

1097
00:52:59,120 --> 00:53:04,120
So, by 1933, 25% of the U.S. population was unemployed.

1098
00:53:04,120 --> 00:53:06,120
And it wasn't just the U.S.

1099
00:53:06,120 --> 00:53:08,120
It was all over the world.

1100
00:53:08,120 --> 00:53:10,120
It was a horrible crisis.

1101
00:53:10,120 --> 00:53:12,620
And we didn't get over

1102
00:53:12,620 --> 00:53:15,120
that crisis until World War II.

1103
00:53:15,120 --> 00:53:17,120
It's like we couldn't get out of it.

1104
00:53:17,120 --> 00:53:20,120
The crisis got so bad that nobody in

1105
00:53:20,120 --> 00:53:23,120
the world could figure out what to do.

1106
00:53:23,120 --> 00:53:27,620
And I think that part of the reason we had World War

1107
00:53:27,620 --> 00:53:32,120
II was because of the anxieties and animosities caused by this massive unemployment.

1108
00:53:32,120 --> 00:53:34,620
Well, we got out of it because

1109
00:53:34,620 --> 00:53:37,120
World War II created a huge stimulus program.

1110
00:53:37,120 --> 00:53:38,620
I mean, they drafted all

1111
00:53:38,620 --> 00:53:40,120
the unemployed and made them fight.

1112
00:53:40,120 --> 00:53:42,120
What an awful outcome.

1113
00:53:42,120 --> 00:53:44,120
But that's what happened.

1114
00:53:44,120 --> 00:53:45,120
It's terrible.

1115
00:53:45,120 --> 00:53:47,620
And so, this time, we saw

1116
00:53:47,620 --> 00:53:50,120
the beginnings of a similar crisis.

1117
00:53:50,120 --> 00:53:52,120
We saw crashes in the stock

1118
00:53:52,120 --> 00:53:54,120
market and the real estate market.

1119
00:53:54,120 --> 00:53:56,120
We saw bankruptcies appearing.

1120
00:53:56,120 --> 00:53:59,120
We saw runs on banks.

1121
00:53:59,120 --> 00:54:02,620
And this time, the government

1122
00:54:02,620 --> 00:54:06,120
decided on a controversial bailout package.

1123
00:54:06,120 --> 00:54:10,120
And so, Ben Bernanke and Mervyn King and other central

1124
00:54:10,120 --> 00:54:14,120
bankers and government policymakers around the world had the idea.

1125
00:54:14,120 --> 00:54:16,120
That we can't let it

1126
00:54:16,120 --> 00:54:18,120
happen the same way this time.

1127
00:54:18,120 --> 00:54:21,120
So, there was massive bailouts.

1128
00:54:21,120 --> 00:54:23,120
Controversial bailouts because they seemed

1129
00:54:23,120 --> 00:54:25,120
to be unfair to many people.

1130
00:54:25,120 --> 00:54:28,120
That's a huge and interesting story.

1131
00:54:28,120 --> 00:54:31,120
I've written three books, by the way, about this crisis.

1132
00:54:31,120 --> 00:54:33,120
Well, two of them with co-authors.

1133
00:54:33,120 --> 00:54:36,120
It's something that fascinates me.

1134
00:54:36,120 --> 00:54:37,620
But I don't want to dwell

1135
00:54:37,620 --> 00:54:39,120
on it too much in this course.

1136
00:54:39,120 --> 00:54:43,120
Because I'm hopeful that it will heal itself.

1137
00:54:43,120 --> 00:54:45,120
And we can put it behind us.

1138
00:54:45,120 --> 00:54:47,620
And the financial crisis doesn't call

1139
00:54:47,620 --> 00:54:50,120
into question the basic principles of finance.

1140
00:54:50,120 --> 00:54:52,120
Not in my mind.

1141
00:54:52,120 --> 00:54:56,620
The vulnerability to a crash that we see in financial markets

1142
00:54:56,620 --> 00:55:01,120
is like the same thing as the vulnerability to crash of airplanes.

1143
00:55:01,120 --> 00:55:03,120
Airplanes crash from time to time.

1144
00:55:03,120 --> 00:55:05,120
You must know that when you get on one.

1145
00:55:05,120 --> 00:55:08,120
But that doesn't mean we shouldn't have airplanes.

1146
00:55:08,120 --> 00:55:12,120
And I think it's the financial system is...

1147
00:55:13,120 --> 00:55:15,620
It's advancing the world with

1148
00:55:15,620 --> 00:55:18,120
such speed and such impressiveness.

1149
00:55:18,120 --> 00:55:20,120
That this crisis is just a

1150
00:55:20,120 --> 00:55:22,120
blip on the screen of that.

1151
00:55:22,120 --> 00:55:27,120
Not something I think we should worry too much about.

1152
00:55:27,120 --> 00:55:29,620
The third lecture is about

1153
00:55:29,620 --> 00:55:32,120
technology and invention in finance.

1154
00:55:32,120 --> 00:55:36,120
Finance is a technology just

1155
00:55:36,120 --> 00:55:40,120
like engineering or mechanical engineering.

1156
00:55:40,120 --> 00:55:42,120
It has principles.

1157
00:55:42,120 --> 00:55:44,120
It has techniques.

1158
00:55:44,120 --> 00:55:47,120
And it involves inventing of details.

1159
00:55:47,120 --> 00:55:50,120
That is, financial institutions are complicated.

1160
00:55:50,120 --> 00:55:53,620
They are complicated the same way automobiles

1161
00:55:53,620 --> 00:55:57,120
or airplanes or nuclear power reactors are.

1162
00:55:57,120 --> 00:56:00,120
You can see this complexity if you read some

1163
00:56:00,120 --> 00:56:03,120
of the documents that are associated with the modern corporation.

1164
00:56:03,120 --> 00:56:05,120
There is a lot there.

1165
00:56:05,120 --> 00:56:07,620
And the way the cash flows

1166
00:56:07,620 --> 00:56:10,120
are divided up among different people.

1167
00:56:10,120 --> 00:56:12,120
Involving options and derivatives.

1168
00:56:12,120 --> 00:56:14,620
And other complicated financial instruments

1169
00:56:14,620 --> 00:56:17,120
are part of the technology.

1170
00:56:17,120 --> 00:56:20,120
And this technology is advancing.

1171
00:56:20,120 --> 00:56:22,120
And it will advance a lot

1172
00:56:22,120 --> 00:56:24,120
over the time of your career.

1173
00:56:24,120 --> 00:56:26,620
I don't have an ability to

1174
00:56:26,620 --> 00:56:29,120
predict the future with any accuracy.

1175
00:56:29,120 --> 00:56:31,120
But I want to try to think

1176
00:56:31,120 --> 00:56:33,120
about what we can say about the future.

1177
00:56:33,120 --> 00:56:37,120
I wrote a book in 2003 called New Financial Order.

1178
00:56:37,120 --> 00:56:39,120
And it was my take on the future.

1179
00:56:39,120 --> 00:56:40,120
But the problem is.

1180
00:56:40,120 --> 00:56:43,120
Nobody really knows the future very well.

1181
00:56:43,120 --> 00:56:47,120
And it's impossible to.

1182
00:56:47,120 --> 00:56:49,120
You kind of have to just invent it

1183
00:56:49,120 --> 00:56:51,120
or dream about what it might be like.

1184
00:56:51,120 --> 00:56:52,120
That's what I did.

1185
00:56:52,120 --> 00:56:55,120
I kind of thought about principles of financial theory.

1186
00:56:55,120 --> 00:56:57,620
And where they might go

1187
00:56:57,620 --> 00:57:00,120
with the advance of information technology.

1188
00:57:00,120 --> 00:57:03,120
And the globalization of the world.

1189
00:57:03,120 --> 00:57:06,120
So, I have just a chapter from that.

1190
00:57:06,120 --> 00:57:08,120
For that section of the course.

1191
00:57:08,120 --> 00:57:13,120
Then, Lecture 4 is about portfolio diversification.

1192
00:57:13,120 --> 00:57:15,120
How risks are spread.

1193
00:57:15,120 --> 00:57:17,120
And we'll talk briefly about

1194
00:57:17,120 --> 00:57:19,120
the capital asset pricing model.

1195
00:57:19,120 --> 00:57:23,120
Now, again, the capital asset pricing

1196
00:57:23,120 --> 00:57:27,120
model is a mathematical theory of diversification.

1197
00:57:27,120 --> 00:57:29,120
A very important theory.

1198
00:57:29,120 --> 00:57:32,120
And it's something that John Giannacopoulos will

1199
00:57:32,120 --> 00:57:35,120
cover with more rigor in Econ 251.

1200
00:57:35,120 --> 00:57:37,120
That I already mentioned.

1201
00:57:37,120 --> 00:57:39,620
But for me, I will talk

1202
00:57:39,620 --> 00:57:42,120
briefly about the capital asset pricing model.

1203
00:57:42,120 --> 00:57:46,120
And our teaching assistant will give a section on it.

1204
00:57:46,120 --> 00:57:48,120
But I want to also think about.

1205
00:57:48,120 --> 00:57:50,120
Since this is a course about the real world.

1206
00:57:50,120 --> 00:57:53,120
I want to think about financial institutions.

1207
00:57:53,120 --> 00:57:55,120
And so many of

1208
00:57:55,120 --> 00:57:57,120
our institutions are offering diversification.

1209
00:57:57,120 --> 00:57:59,120
One way or another.

1210
00:57:59,120 --> 00:58:01,120
And so, again, I wanted to talk

1211
00:58:01,120 --> 00:58:03,120
about the real world component of this.

1212
00:58:03,120 --> 00:58:06,120
The fifth lecture is about insurance.

1213
00:58:06,120 --> 00:58:15,120
And the insurance industry developed over the centuries.

1214
00:58:15,120 --> 00:58:17,120
It goes actually all the way back to ancient Rome.

1215
00:58:17,120 --> 00:58:19,120
But only minimally.

1216
00:58:19,120 --> 00:58:23,120
People didn't have the concepts until the 1600s.

1217
00:58:23,120 --> 00:58:26,120
When probability theory was invented.

1218
00:58:26,120 --> 00:58:28,120
There was an intuitive concept.

1219
00:58:28,120 --> 00:58:30,120
Sure, I could start an insurance company.

1220
00:58:30,120 --> 00:58:32,120
I could put together a lot

1221
00:58:32,120 --> 00:58:34,120
of insurance policies and charge for them.

1222
00:58:34,120 --> 00:58:36,120
Probably I won't.

1223
00:58:36,120 --> 00:58:38,620
You know, some intuitive sense about law

1224
00:58:38,620 --> 00:58:41,120
of large number or independence of risk.

1225
00:58:41,120 --> 00:58:43,120
Probably I'll be okay.

1226
00:58:43,120 --> 00:58:45,120
And I can make good on the policies I wrote.

1227
00:58:45,120 --> 00:58:47,120
But it was never clear

1228
00:58:47,120 --> 00:58:49,120
until probability theory was developed.

1229
00:58:49,120 --> 00:58:51,120
Since then, it's been growing.

1230
00:58:51,120 --> 00:58:52,120
And it's becoming a bigger

1231
00:58:52,120 --> 00:58:53,120
and bigger part of our lives.

1232
00:58:53,120 --> 00:58:59,120
And I think that insurance is actually a lifesaver.

1233
00:58:59,120 --> 00:59:01,120
I'll give you one example.

1234
00:59:01,120 --> 00:59:03,120
You know that in the earthquake in Haiti.

1235
00:59:03,120 --> 00:59:07,120
What was that?

1236
00:59:07,120 --> 00:59:09,120
About a year ago.

1237
00:59:09,120 --> 00:59:13,120
There was a tremendous loss of life.

1238
00:59:13,120 --> 00:59:16,620
But the earthquake in San Francisco,

1239
00:59:16,620 --> 00:59:20,120
decades earlier, was of the same magnitude.

1240
00:59:20,120 --> 00:59:22,120
And had very little loss of life.

1241
00:59:22,120 --> 00:59:27,120
Also, the loss suffered by people in terms of destruction of

1242
00:59:27,120 --> 00:59:32,120
their homes and their office buildings was vastly higher in Haiti.

1243
00:59:32,120 --> 00:59:35,620
And it turns out that Haiti, a less developed

1244
00:59:35,620 --> 00:59:39,120
country, didn't have much of the modern insurance industry.

1245
00:59:39,120 --> 00:59:41,120
So that people were uninsured against

1246
00:59:41,120 --> 00:59:43,120
risk of collapse of their structure.

1247
00:59:43,120 --> 00:59:45,620
And you didn't have insurance industries

1248
00:59:45,620 --> 00:59:48,120
going in and policing building codes.

1249
00:59:48,120 --> 00:59:52,120
If the insurance company is liable to the risk, then they

1250
00:59:52,120 --> 00:59:56,120
go in and say, we won't insure you unless you fix this.

1251
00:59:56,120 --> 01:00:00,120
Since it didn't happen, so many people died.

1252
01:00:00,120 --> 01:00:02,120
I think that Haiti will come along.

1253
01:00:02,120 --> 01:00:06,120
We have a Caribbean insurance initiative that was starting.

1254
01:00:06,120 --> 01:00:11,120
We want to see the developing world get these institutions.

1255
01:00:11,120 --> 01:00:12,620
I want to try to give

1256
01:00:12,620 --> 01:00:14,120
a sense of the reality of that.

1257
01:00:14,120 --> 01:00:16,120
We tend to think of Haiti

1258
01:00:16,120 --> 01:00:18,120
as an opportunity for our charity.

1259
01:00:18,120 --> 01:00:19,620
And a lot of us

1260
01:00:19,620 --> 01:00:21,120
gave money to help these people.

1261
01:00:21,120 --> 01:00:26,120
But charity doesn't work on a big enough scale.

1262
01:00:26,120 --> 01:00:29,120
Going around to people on the street and asking

1263
01:00:29,120 --> 01:00:32,120
them to give money to help the Haitian earthquake victims.

1264
01:00:32,120 --> 01:00:34,120
It doesn't amount to a lot.

1265
01:00:34,120 --> 01:00:37,620
What really becomes big and important is the insurance industry,

1266
01:00:37,620 --> 01:00:41,120
which is doing the same thing as a business model.

1267
01:00:41,120 --> 01:00:44,120
And that's the real world.

1268
01:00:44,120 --> 01:00:47,120
And it matters enormously.

1269
01:00:47,120 --> 01:00:51,120
The sixth lecture is about efficient markets.

1270
01:00:51,120 --> 01:00:57,120
This is about a theory that developed in the 1960s.

1271
01:00:57,120 --> 01:01:00,120
That financial markets are wonderfully perfect.

1272
01:01:00,120 --> 01:01:01,120
I'm saying.

1273
01:01:01,120 --> 01:01:03,120
I'm a little bit skeptical of this theory,

1274
01:01:03,120 --> 01:01:05,120
although I think it has an element of truth.

1275
01:01:05,120 --> 01:01:08,620
Efficient markets theory is the idea that you

1276
01:01:08,620 --> 01:01:12,120
really can't make money by trading in financial markets.

1277
01:01:12,120 --> 01:01:16,120
Because the markets are so competitive that the

1278
01:01:16,120 --> 01:01:20,120
price is always pushed to an optimal level

1279
01:01:20,120 --> 01:01:22,620
that incorporates all information that anyone

1280
01:01:22,620 --> 01:01:25,120
could ever have about the security.

1281
01:01:25,120 --> 01:01:27,120
And the theory has been that

1282
01:01:27,120 --> 01:01:29,120
it's hopeless to try to invest.

1283
01:01:29,120 --> 01:01:32,120
And beat the market.

1284
01:01:32,120 --> 01:01:35,120
Well, I think there's an element of truth to that.

1285
01:01:35,120 --> 01:01:37,120
But it's not quite true.

1286
01:01:37,120 --> 01:01:40,120
And people like David Swenson are counter examples.

1287
01:01:40,120 --> 01:01:43,120
That it is possible for professional

1288
01:01:43,120 --> 01:01:46,120
money managers to beat the market.

1289
01:01:46,120 --> 01:01:49,620
And that's something I want to think

1290
01:01:49,620 --> 01:01:53,120
about and talk about in that lecture.

1291
01:01:53,120 --> 01:01:56,120
Lecture seven is about debt markets.

1292
01:01:56,120 --> 01:01:57,120
And it's about.

1293
01:01:57,120 --> 01:02:03,120
We have a lot of money that's lent.

1294
01:02:03,120 --> 01:02:07,120
The Federal Reserve manages these markets.

1295
01:02:07,120 --> 01:02:12,620
It tries to coordinate the markets through open market

1296
01:02:12,620 --> 01:02:18,120
operations and through what now is called quantitative easing.

1297
01:02:18,120 --> 01:02:21,120
But the markets are huge and international.

1298
01:02:21,120 --> 01:02:24,120
They involve errors that people make.

1299
01:02:24,120 --> 01:02:26,120
A lot of people get overly indebted.

1300
01:02:26,120 --> 01:02:29,120
And make mistakes over their lives.

1301
01:02:29,120 --> 01:02:32,120
But they also offer opportunities.

1302
01:02:32,120 --> 01:02:34,120
That debt markets are fundamental to the

1303
01:02:34,120 --> 01:02:36,120
things we want to do in our lives.

1304
01:02:36,120 --> 01:02:38,620
For example, when you are a little bit older,

1305
01:02:38,620 --> 01:02:41,120
many of you will want to buy a house.

1306
01:02:41,120 --> 01:02:42,120
Right?

1307
01:02:42,120 --> 01:02:44,120
But you won't be in that point in the life

1308
01:02:44,120 --> 01:02:46,120
cycle when you have the money to buy a house.

1309
01:02:46,120 --> 01:02:47,120
Most of you.

1310
01:02:47,120 --> 01:02:48,120
So you'll be borrowing.

1311
01:02:48,120 --> 01:02:49,120
It's elementary.

1312
01:02:49,120 --> 01:02:50,120
You take out a mortgage.

1313
01:02:50,120 --> 01:02:51,120
That seems obvious.

1314
01:02:51,120 --> 01:02:52,620
But still today in many countries of the

1315
01:02:52,620 --> 01:02:54,120
world, the mortgage market is not very developed.

1316
01:02:54,120 --> 01:02:55,120
And that's why.

1317
01:02:55,120 --> 01:02:56,120
You can't do that.

1318
01:02:56,120 --> 01:02:57,620
So there's a good side to

1319
01:02:57,620 --> 01:02:59,120
borrowing as well as a bad side.

1320
01:02:59,120 --> 01:03:01,120
I want to put it in perspective.

1321
01:03:01,120 --> 01:03:04,620
Again, our review session will talk a little bit

1322
01:03:04,620 --> 01:03:08,120
somewhat with our teaching assistant about the mathematics of debt.

1323
01:03:08,120 --> 01:03:11,120
Lecture 8 will be about the stock market.

1324
01:03:11,120 --> 01:03:13,120
Again, I think of the stock market

1325
01:03:13,120 --> 01:03:15,120
not as something that we're going to beat.

1326
01:03:15,120 --> 01:03:19,120
I think it's something that is

1327
01:03:19,120 --> 01:03:23,120
an invention to motivate people to borrow.

1328
01:03:23,120 --> 01:03:25,620
It's an invention to motivate

1329
01:03:25,620 --> 01:03:28,120
people to get people working together.

1330
01:03:28,120 --> 01:03:31,120
So the basic idea of a stock investment.

1331
01:03:31,120 --> 01:03:34,120
You and your friends want to set up a company.

1332
01:03:34,120 --> 01:03:35,120
Okay?

1333
01:03:35,120 --> 01:03:36,120
How do you do that?

1334
01:03:36,120 --> 01:03:38,120
Well, the company needs money to start.

1335
01:03:38,120 --> 01:03:40,620
So if somebody's got to contribute capital, well, some

1336
01:03:40,620 --> 01:03:43,120
of you have more money to contribute than others.

1337
01:03:43,120 --> 01:03:45,120
So you should have a bigger share in the company.

1338
01:03:45,120 --> 01:03:47,620
Some of you have no money at all to

1339
01:03:47,620 --> 01:03:50,120
contribute, but you're going to contribute your time and energy.

1340
01:03:50,120 --> 01:03:51,620
So you want to give a share

1341
01:03:51,620 --> 01:03:53,120
in the company to these other people.

1342
01:03:53,120 --> 01:03:55,620
And you want to set up a company to

1343
01:03:55,620 --> 01:03:58,120
set up as well in order to incentivize them.

1344
01:03:58,120 --> 01:04:01,620
So you devise a whole scheme to set

1345
01:04:01,620 --> 01:04:05,120
up a company that involves the creation of stock.

1346
01:04:05,120 --> 01:04:07,120
And then you start trading the stock.

1347
01:04:07,120 --> 01:04:09,120
And then it gets all the more interesting.

1348
01:04:09,120 --> 01:04:12,120
And then there are options on these stock certificates.

1349
01:04:12,120 --> 01:04:13,120
But it's all for a purpose.

1350
01:04:13,120 --> 01:04:17,120
The purpose is to make some enterprise happen.

1351
01:04:17,120 --> 01:04:21,120
And it really is important that we have these institutions.

1352
01:04:21,120 --> 01:04:23,120
Because if you don't have them,

1353
01:04:23,120 --> 01:04:25,120
you're not going to be able to do anything.

1354
01:04:25,120 --> 01:04:26,120
It's going to fall apart.

1355
01:04:26,120 --> 01:04:28,120
Someone's going to get angry and leave.

1356
01:04:28,120 --> 01:04:30,120
It's just not going to work.

1357
01:04:30,120 --> 01:04:31,620
And so I think of the

1358
01:04:31,620 --> 01:04:33,120
stock market as doing these functions.

1359
01:04:33,120 --> 01:04:35,620
Now, I know Karl Marx said he thought it

1360
01:04:35,620 --> 01:04:38,120
was a big casino, but we're not communists here.

1361
01:04:38,120 --> 01:04:41,120
This is about modern finance.

1362
01:04:41,120 --> 01:04:44,120
Lecture 9 is about real estate.

1363
01:04:44,120 --> 01:04:46,620
And another fascination for me, I've

1364
01:04:46,620 --> 01:04:49,120
been working for years about real estate.

1365
01:04:49,120 --> 01:04:50,620
And in fact, I have my

1366
01:04:50,620 --> 01:04:52,120
own with my colleague, Karl Case.

1367
01:04:52,120 --> 01:04:54,120
He's a real estate investor.

1368
01:04:54,120 --> 01:04:56,620
And he's been working on a lot of home price

1369
01:04:56,620 --> 01:04:59,120
indices called the Standard & Poor's Case-Shiller home price indices.

1370
01:04:59,120 --> 01:05:01,120
We'll talk about those.

1371
01:05:01,120 --> 01:05:03,120
But it's really important for

1372
01:05:03,120 --> 01:05:05,120
this crisis that we've just seen.

1373
01:05:05,120 --> 01:05:08,620
Because the financial crisis was caused

1374
01:05:08,620 --> 01:05:12,120
substantially by a bubble in home prices.

1375
01:05:12,120 --> 01:05:16,620
I believe a psychologically induced excitement or euphoria about home prices

1376
01:05:16,620 --> 01:05:21,120
in the United States and in other countries that collapsed around 2006.

1377
01:05:21,120 --> 01:05:22,620
And the real estate market is

1378
01:05:22,620 --> 01:05:24,120
starting to get more and more important.

1379
01:05:24,120 --> 01:05:26,120
And it's starting to get more and more

1380
01:05:26,120 --> 01:05:28,120
important in other parts of the world more recently.

1381
01:05:28,120 --> 01:05:31,120
And the real estate market is

1382
01:05:31,120 --> 01:05:34,120
getting very speculative and psychological, I believe.

1383
01:05:34,120 --> 01:05:37,120
And the outlook right now for the

1384
01:05:37,120 --> 01:05:40,120
economy hinges on how these markets behave.

1385
01:05:40,120 --> 01:05:44,120
So, that will be, I think,

1386
01:05:44,120 --> 01:05:48,120
an important lecture for this course.

1387
01:05:48,120 --> 01:05:50,120
Lecture 10 is about behavioral finance.

1388
01:05:50,120 --> 01:05:52,120
I talked about that.

1389
01:05:52,120 --> 01:05:56,120
It's another long-standing interest of mine to

1390
01:05:56,120 --> 01:06:00,120
try to incorporate psychology into our theory.

1391
01:06:00,120 --> 01:06:05,120
So, lecture 12 is about banking, multiple

1392
01:06:05,120 --> 01:06:10,120
expansion of credit, money multiplier, and bank regulation.

1393
01:06:10,120 --> 01:06:13,120
Which is something that is a fascinating topic.

1394
01:06:13,120 --> 01:06:16,120
Because we almost lost our banking system.

1395
01:06:16,120 --> 01:06:18,120
We had to bail them out massively.

1396
01:06:18,120 --> 01:06:20,120
We have international loans.

1397
01:06:20,120 --> 01:06:22,120
We have international accords now.

1398
01:06:22,120 --> 01:06:25,120
Notably, a new one just came out called Basel

1399
01:06:25,120 --> 01:06:28,120
III from Basel, which is a city in Switzerland.

1400
01:06:28,120 --> 01:06:30,620
And it was endorsed by the G20

1401
01:06:30,620 --> 01:06:33,120
countries at their Korean meeting in Seoul.

1402
01:06:33,120 --> 01:06:38,620
So, we're seeing a change in bank regulation that will, we

1403
01:06:38,620 --> 01:06:44,120
hope, prevent another crisis like the one we just went through.

1404
01:06:44,120 --> 01:06:48,120
Lecture 13 is about forwards and futures markets.

1405
01:06:48,120 --> 01:06:51,620
Futures markets, well, forward markets are markets

1406
01:06:51,620 --> 01:06:55,120
for contracts that deliver in the future.

1407
01:06:55,120 --> 01:07:00,120
Over-the-counter contracts, they're called, that are done one-on-one

1408
01:07:00,120 --> 01:07:05,120
between parties with the help of an investment bank.

1409
01:07:05,120 --> 01:07:09,620
Or futures contracts, which are traded on

1410
01:07:09,620 --> 01:07:14,120
organized futures exchange, like the Chicago Mercantile Exchange.

1411
01:07:14,120 --> 01:07:16,120
I have some involvement with this.

1412
01:07:16,120 --> 01:07:20,620
Because we worked with the Chicago Mercantile Exchange to create

1413
01:07:20,620 --> 01:07:25,120
a futures market for single-family homes using the S&P Case-Shiller Index.

1414
01:07:25,120 --> 01:07:27,120
So, I'm involved in this.

1415
01:07:27,120 --> 01:07:30,120
And we have that market

1416
01:07:30,120 --> 01:07:33,120
functioning at a rather low level.

1417
01:07:33,120 --> 01:07:36,120
But it is functioning and seems to be growing lately.

1418
01:07:36,120 --> 01:07:38,120
I'm hopeful for that market.

1419
01:07:38,120 --> 01:07:41,120
Lecture 14 is about options markets.

1420
01:07:41,120 --> 01:07:45,120
These are most typically stock options, which are common.

1421
01:07:45,120 --> 01:07:49,620
These are contracts that allow you to purchase a share of

1422
01:07:49,620 --> 01:07:54,120
a stock or to sell a share at a pre-specified price.

1423
01:07:54,120 --> 01:07:56,120
These are traded on options exchanges.

1424
01:07:56,120 --> 01:07:58,120
They have a price that goes up and down.

1425
01:07:58,120 --> 01:08:02,620
This is an example of a derivative contract

1426
01:08:02,620 --> 01:08:07,120
that injects a lot of complexity into financial theory.

1427
01:08:07,120 --> 01:08:10,120
Lecture 15 is about monetary policy.

1428
01:08:10,120 --> 01:08:13,120
It's about the central banks of the world.

1429
01:08:13,120 --> 01:08:15,120
For example, our central bank, called the Federal Reserve.

1430
01:08:15,120 --> 01:08:17,120
In the United States.

1431
01:08:17,120 --> 01:08:20,620
And it's about what they do and how they

1432
01:08:20,620 --> 01:08:24,120
help prevent crises like the one we've just seen.

1433
01:08:24,120 --> 01:08:26,120
They did help prevent it.

1434
01:08:26,120 --> 01:08:28,120
I think they saved off disaster.

1435
01:08:28,120 --> 01:08:31,120
Lecture 16 is about investment banking.

1436
01:08:31,120 --> 01:08:34,120
I know this is of great interest because we place

1437
01:08:34,120 --> 01:08:37,120
a lot of students in good jobs in investment banking.

1438
01:08:37,120 --> 01:08:41,120
Companies like Goldman Sachs, most talked about one.

1439
01:08:41,120 --> 01:08:43,120
These help investment bankers help them.

1440
01:08:43,120 --> 01:08:44,120
They help them.

1441
01:08:44,120 --> 01:08:47,620
Investment bankers help companies raise

1442
01:08:47,620 --> 01:08:51,120
capital, issue securities, retire securities.

1443
01:08:51,120 --> 01:08:54,120
And we want to talk about how they're regulated.

1444
01:08:54,120 --> 01:08:56,120
And I didn't mention Dodd-Frank, by the way.

1445
01:08:56,120 --> 01:09:00,620
We have a new bill that just passed in July in

1446
01:09:00,620 --> 01:09:05,120
the United States that changes the regulatory structure for investment banks

1447
01:09:05,120 --> 01:09:08,120
and a whole array of financial institutions.

1448
01:09:08,120 --> 01:09:10,120
And I want to talk about that.

1449
01:09:10,120 --> 01:09:12,120
It's, there are other, in other countries.

1450
01:09:12,120 --> 01:09:16,120
The European Union, the European Parliament has created a

1451
01:09:16,120 --> 01:09:20,120
number of new laws and organizations that somewhat resemble Dodd-Frank.

1452
01:09:20,120 --> 01:09:24,120
And other countries have also done financial regulation reform

1453
01:09:24,120 --> 01:09:28,120
that affects investment banking and other aspects of finance.

1454
01:09:28,120 --> 01:09:30,120
It's extremely complicated.

1455
01:09:30,120 --> 01:09:33,620
And I don't want to give you too many details, but I

1456
01:09:33,620 --> 01:09:37,120
want to give you some sense of the revolution that we're seeing.

1457
01:09:37,120 --> 01:09:39,120
Lecture 17 is about professional

1458
01:09:39,120 --> 01:09:41,120
money managers like David Swenson.

1459
01:09:41,120 --> 01:09:43,120
People who manage portfolios.

1460
01:09:43,120 --> 01:09:45,620
You don't have to be a

1461
01:09:45,620 --> 01:09:48,120
billionaire to manage a billion dollar portfolio.

1462
01:09:48,120 --> 01:09:51,120
In fact, some of you may be doing it sooner

1463
01:09:51,120 --> 01:09:54,120
than you realize if you get the right kind of job.

1464
01:09:54,120 --> 01:09:56,620
Managing a portfolio means managing the

1465
01:09:56,620 --> 01:09:59,120
risks, putting them in the right places.

1466
01:09:59,120 --> 01:10:04,120
And also, you know, you think of institutional investors,

1467
01:10:04,120 --> 01:10:09,120
big money managers as just trying to make money.

1468
01:10:09,120 --> 01:10:11,120
But when you get into that field, you're not.

1469
01:10:11,120 --> 01:10:14,120
When you get into that field, you realize that you

1470
01:10:14,120 --> 01:10:17,120
are deciding on, you have power as an institutional investor.

1471
01:10:17,120 --> 01:10:20,120
You can go to, when you own a big share of some

1472
01:10:20,120 --> 01:10:23,120
company, you can go to the board meeting and talk to these people.

1473
01:10:23,120 --> 01:10:26,120
And you, or the stockholders meeting, and you'll get heard

1474
01:10:26,120 --> 01:10:29,120
if you own 10% of the shares of a company.

1475
01:10:29,120 --> 01:10:31,120
Then you suddenly realize that you

1476
01:10:31,120 --> 01:10:33,120
are a steward of the public interest.

1477
01:10:33,120 --> 01:10:35,120
And I think institutional investors

1478
01:10:35,120 --> 01:10:37,120
are recognizing that more and more.

1479
01:10:37,120 --> 01:10:39,120
Lecture 18 is about exchanges.

1480
01:10:39,120 --> 01:10:41,120
Brokers, dealers, clearing houses.

1481
01:10:41,120 --> 01:10:43,120
Like the New York Stock

1482
01:10:43,120 --> 01:10:45,120
Exchange or the London Stock Exchange.

1483
01:10:45,120 --> 01:10:47,120
They are proliferating around the world.

1484
01:10:47,120 --> 01:10:50,120
Whereas there were just a few 30 years

1485
01:10:50,120 --> 01:10:53,120
ago, now almost every country has a stock exchange.

1486
01:10:53,120 --> 01:10:56,120
And a complicated list of exchanges.

1487
01:10:56,120 --> 01:10:58,120
They are increasingly electronic.

1488
01:10:58,120 --> 01:11:00,620
They have interesting new features

1489
01:11:00,620 --> 01:11:03,120
like microsecond trading that's going on.

1490
01:11:03,120 --> 01:11:06,120
Computers trading with other computers.

1491
01:11:06,120 --> 01:11:09,120
We'll talk about where this is going.

1492
01:11:09,120 --> 01:11:11,120
Lecture 19 is about the market.

1493
01:11:11,120 --> 01:11:15,120
And I'm going to talk about public and nonprofit finance.

1494
01:11:15,120 --> 01:11:17,120
So, I think this is very important.

1495
01:11:17,120 --> 01:11:21,120
Nonprofit finance would include organizations like Yale University

1496
01:11:21,120 --> 01:11:25,120
or churches and charities and other things like that.

1497
01:11:25,120 --> 01:11:28,120
But I'm also including in this lecture public finance.

1498
01:11:28,120 --> 01:11:31,120
And that means governments financing projects.

1499
01:11:31,120 --> 01:11:34,120
So, for example, you take it for granted

1500
01:11:34,120 --> 01:11:37,120
that our city here in New Haven has roads.

1501
01:11:37,120 --> 01:11:38,120
It has schools.

1502
01:11:38,120 --> 01:11:39,120
It has sewers.

1503
01:11:39,120 --> 01:11:40,120
It has water.

1504
01:11:40,120 --> 01:11:42,120
All this kind of comes without your even asking.

1505
01:11:42,120 --> 01:11:44,120
But all of these things had to be financed.

1506
01:11:44,120 --> 01:11:46,120
And the city of New Haven,

1507
01:11:46,120 --> 01:11:48,120
like other cities, is issuing debt.

1508
01:11:48,120 --> 01:11:51,120
And it's a complicated business.

1509
01:11:51,120 --> 01:11:53,120
I want to get you into

1510
01:11:53,120 --> 01:11:55,120
some of the details because it matters.

1511
01:11:55,120 --> 01:11:57,120
Because this is how you make things happen.

1512
01:11:57,120 --> 01:12:00,620
You can go to your city government and you can

1513
01:12:00,620 --> 01:12:04,120
propose that they issue revenue bonds to start some new product.

1514
01:12:04,120 --> 01:12:05,120
You would know.

1515
01:12:05,120 --> 01:12:06,620
That's what I want you to do

1516
01:12:06,620 --> 01:12:08,120
is to know how these things are done.

1517
01:12:08,120 --> 01:12:10,120
So, that it's not just imagination.

1518
01:12:10,120 --> 01:12:12,120
You can make it happen.

1519
01:12:12,120 --> 01:12:14,120
And also nonprofits.

1520
01:12:14,120 --> 01:12:16,120
I want you to understand that

1521
01:12:16,120 --> 01:12:18,120
you can set up your own nonprofit.

1522
01:12:18,120 --> 01:12:20,120
And there's a lot of advantages to doing that.

1523
01:12:20,120 --> 01:12:22,620
That's an organization that has

1524
01:12:22,620 --> 01:12:25,120
a financial structure, but no shareholders.

1525
01:12:25,120 --> 01:12:27,120
Nobody takes home the money.

1526
01:12:27,120 --> 01:12:29,120
It all goes to some cause.

1527
01:12:29,120 --> 01:12:32,620
And finally, my last lecture, Lecture 20, I'm

1528
01:12:32,620 --> 01:12:36,120
calling it the Finding Your Purpose in Finance.

1529
01:12:36,120 --> 01:12:37,620
I just want to come back in the last

1530
01:12:37,620 --> 01:12:39,120
lecture to the idea that this is a concept.

1531
01:12:39,120 --> 01:12:42,120
The idea that this is a

1532
01:12:42,120 --> 01:12:45,120
course about, not about making money.

1533
01:12:45,120 --> 01:12:49,620
I don't want you to give a billion dollars to your

1534
01:12:49,620 --> 01:12:54,120
children and grandchildren, which they will then squander in conspicuous consumption.

1535
01:12:54,120 --> 01:12:58,120
The idea is a moral purpose.

1536
01:12:58,120 --> 01:13:01,620
And that's one thing I wanted to try to convey, partly

1537
01:13:01,620 --> 01:13:05,120
with outside speakers, maybe with other examples that I can give.

1538
01:13:05,120 --> 01:13:07,120
That I think that many people who are wealthy,

1539
01:13:07,120 --> 01:13:08,120
who are not wealthy,

1540
01:13:08,120 --> 01:13:12,120
and who have succeeded in finance,

1541
01:13:12,120 --> 01:13:17,120
really don't care about spending the money on themselves.

1542
01:13:17,120 --> 01:13:19,120
They really do have a purpose.

1543
01:13:19,120 --> 01:13:21,120
Even if that's not true of many of them.

1544
01:13:21,120 --> 01:13:23,620
There's an interesting book by Robert Frank, I don't

1545
01:13:23,620 --> 01:13:26,120
have it on the reading list, called Riches Stan,

1546
01:13:26,120 --> 01:13:29,120
who talks about what wealthy people are like these days.

1547
01:13:29,120 --> 01:13:32,620
And if you read his book, sometimes they are

1548
01:13:32,620 --> 01:13:36,120
disgustingly rich and spending the money on silly things.

1549
01:13:36,120 --> 01:13:38,120
But there is a, there is a,

1550
01:13:38,120 --> 01:13:41,120
there is an idea among many of them,

1551
01:13:41,120 --> 01:13:43,620
that they are going to do

1552
01:13:43,620 --> 01:13:46,120
their good things for the world.

1553
01:13:46,120 --> 01:13:48,120
And I think that's what I,

1554
01:13:48,120 --> 01:13:49,620
that's, because I think many

1555
01:13:49,620 --> 01:13:51,120
of you will do these things.

1556
01:13:51,120 --> 01:13:52,620
I want to think about

1557
01:13:52,620 --> 01:13:54,120
the purpose that you'll find in,

1558
01:13:54,120 --> 01:13:56,120
in,

1559
01:13:56,120 --> 01:13:58,120
in,

1560
01:13:58,120 --> 01:14:00,120
in finance.

1561
01:14:00,120 --> 01:14:02,120
So, that's just the closing thought.

1562
01:14:02,120 --> 01:14:03,120
I'll leave you,

1563
01:14:03,120 --> 01:14:06,120
I'll see you again on Wednesday.

1564
01:14:06,120 --> 01:14:07,120
But the closing thought is that,

1565
01:14:07,120 --> 01:14:11,120
it is about making your purposes happen.

